# A template's `content/` tree is copied byte for byte over a freshly written project,
# so whatever git hands back on checkout is literally what an author receives. Line-ending
# normalisation has no business in the middle of that: it would mean the template someone
# gets depends on their `core.autocrlf`, and that the thing shipped is not the thing tested.
* -text

# Said outright for the asset store as well, because there the damage is silent rather
# than cosmetic: it holds raw bytes under extension-less, content-addressed names, git has
# to guess text-vs-binary for those, and a wrong guess rewrites line endings inside a JPEG
# or an Ogg. The file still checks out — but its content no longer hashes to the name it is
# filed under, and the asset just fails to load.
*/content/assets/content/** binary
